Q: Integrate Countly SDK Web with TypeScript A: <p>Adding a file like <em>countly-sdk-web.d.ts</em> might help:</p>
<pre class="lang-typescript prettyprint-override"><code>declare module 'countly-sdk-web' {
    interface CountlyConfig {
        app_key: string;
        url: string;
        [key: string]: any; // For additional optional properties
    }

    interface Countly {
        init: (config: CountlyConfig) =&gt; void;
        track_events: boolean;
        [key: string]: any; // For other properties and methods
    }

    const Countly: Countly;
    export default Countly;
}
</code></pre>
<p>And then import like:</p>
<pre class="lang-typescript prettyprint-override"><code>import Countly from 'countly-sdk-web';
</code></pre>

Q: Using count_all_results or get_compiled_select and $this-&gt;db-&gt;get(&#39;table&#39;) lists table twice in query? A: <p>The code in your question is struggling to manage the query builder cache.</p>
<h3>Your query #1:</h3>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$this-&gt;db-&gt;join('tblPlots', 'tblPlots.programID=tblProgram.pkProgramID');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;join('tblTrees', 'tblTrees.treePlotID=tblPlots.id');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;order_by('tblTrees.id', 'ASC');
$allResults = $this-&gt;db-&gt;count_all_results('tblProgram', false);
</code></pre>
<p>This executes a <code>SELECT COUNT(*)</code> query with a FROM, two JOINs, and an ORDER BY clause. The false parameter on count_all_results() preserves the built clauses.</p>
<h3>Your query #2 (the first loop iteration):</h3>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$this-&gt;db-&gt;limit($chunk, $offset);  
$result = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get('tblProgram')-&gt;result_array();
</code></pre>
<p>This adds a LIMIT, adds an OFFSET (if not zero), and adds another table to the FROM clause to the original query, then executes the query. If you would have called get() with no parameters, the extra table wouldn't be added. In fact, the limit() call can be absorbed into the get() call like this:</p>
<p><code>$this-&gt;db-&gt;get('', $limit, $offset)</code></p>
<h3>Your query #3 (and beyond):</h3>
<p>Because your second query reset the query builder after execution, all of the JOIN and ORDER BY logic is erased before this new query is executed. In other words, your new query will be:</p>
<p><code>SELECT * FROM `tblProgram` LIMIT 2000 OFFSET 4000</code></p>
<hr />
<p>I recommend that immediately after building the core logic of your query, declare a couple clones of the query builder object for subsequent use.</p>
<p>Remember that when you call get() on a query builder object, all of the clauses built in that object/cache will be erased.</p>
<hr />
<p>Ignoring your performance issue and the refactored script in your answer, the general approach should resemble this:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function get_download_tree_data($options = [], $rand = ''): array
{
    $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;from('tblProgram')
        -&gt;join('tblPlots', 'tblPlots.programID=tblProgram.pkProgramID')
        -&gt;join('tblTrees', 'tblTrees.treePlotID=tblPlots.id');

    $lastingClone = clone $this-&gt;db;
    $consumableClone = clone $this-&gt;db;

    $allResults = $this-&gt;db-&gt;count_all_results();

    //chunk data and write to CSV to avoid reaching memory limit
    $offset = 0;
    $chunk = 2000;
    $treePath = $this-&gt;config-&gt;item('temp_path') . &quot;$rand/trees.csv&quot;;
    $tree_handle = fopen($treePath, 'a');
    while ($offset &lt; $allResults) {
        $result = $consumableClone
            -&gt;order_by('tblTrees.id')
            -&gt;get('', $chunk, $offset)
            -&gt;result_array();
        foreach ($result as $row) {
            fputcsv($tree_handle, $row);
        }    
        $offset += $chunk;
        $consumableClone = clone $lastingClone;
    }  
    fclose($tree_handle);
    return ['resultCount' =&gt; $allResults];
}
</code></pre>
